%S A402311 1,1,1,7,2,1,2,1,1,2,1,1,6,1,1,7,1,5,1,11,20,2,19,4,1,1,4,1,65,8,4,4,
%T A402311 1,2,1,30,24,2,6,4,8,8,6,186,2,2,1,12,6,12,4,28
%N A402311 Narrow class numbers associated with terms of A401222.
%C A402311 a(n) is the narrow class number of the real quadratic field K = Q(sqrt(A401222(n))). This means that a(n) = A401260(n) if the fundamental unit of K has norm -1 and 2*A401260(n) if the fundamental unit of K has norm 1.
%C A402311 In particular (see the Ezra Brown link below):
%C A402311 (i) If A401260(n) is prime, then a(n) = A401260(n);
%C A402311 (ii) If A401260(n) has a prime factor congruent to 3 modulo 4, then a(n) = 2*A401260(n);
%C A402311 (iii) If A401260(n) = p*q, where p == q == 1 (mod 4), Legendre(p,q) = Legendre(q,p) = -1, then a(n) = A401260(n).
%H A402311 Ezra Brown, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1090/S0002-9947-1974-0364172-9">Class numbers of real quadratic number fields</a>, Trans. Amer. Math. Soc. 190 (1974), 99-107.
%H A402311 D. H. Lehmer, E. Lehmer and D. Shanks, <a href="/A002189/a002189.pdf">Integer sequences having prescribed quadratic character</a>, Math. Comp., 24 (1970), 433-451 [Annotated scanned copy].
